# Canvas

CSS-first design system for the Olympus platform.
Published as `@olympusoss/canvas` on npm (v3.0.0+).

## What Canvas is

Canvas is a pure CSS design system with optional JS utilities. It ships
modular CSS files and small framework-agnostic JS helpers. It contains no
React, React Native, Vue, or other framework code.

Framework-specific component libraries live in dedicated downstream
packages that depend on Canvas:

- `@olympusoss/canvas-react`: React components for web.
- `@olympusoss/canvas-react-native`: React Native components for mobile.
- `@olympusoss/canvas-vue`: Vue components.
- `@olympusoss/canvas-flux`: Flux components.
- Additional bindings follow the pattern `@olympusoss/canvas-{framework}`.

The dependency arrow is strictly one-way: downstream packages depend on
Canvas; Canvas never depends on or references a downstream package.

Styles are authored as semantic CSS with custom properties.

## Architecture

### CSS

- **Custom properties are the API.** Every visual decision (color, spacing,
  typography, radius, shadow, motion) is a CSS custom property. Components
  reference properties, never raw values.
- **CSS layers for specificity control.** Five layers in strict order:
  `canvas.reset`, `canvas.tokens`, `canvas.base`, `canvas.components`,
  `canvas.patterns`. See "CSS layers" below.
- **Modular imports.** Each concern ships as its own CSS file. Consumers
  import only what they use (a convenience all-in-one entry point may exist).
- **No preprocessors.** Author plain CSS. Custom properties, `@layer`,
  `@container`, `color-mix()`, `light-dark()`, and modern CSS features
  replace what Sass/Less used to provide.
- **Theming via custom properties.** Light theme at `:root`, dark theme via
  `.dark` on `<html>`. Components never change; only token values do.
  Glass surface and density are additional attribute-driven layers.

### JS utilities

Small, focused helpers for what CSS genuinely cannot do:

- Theme switching (detecting preference, toggling, persisting).
- Token access (reading computed property values from JS when needed).
- Class composition (a `cn()` or similar merge utility if warranted).

No framework dependencies. Works in any environment that has a DOM.

### CSS layers

The cascade is controlled by five named layers, declared in this order:

```
@layer canvas.reset, canvas.tokens, canvas.base, canvas.components, canvas.patterns;
```

- `canvas.reset`: browser normalization (box-sizing, margin/padding reset).
- `canvas.tokens`: custom property definitions (colors, typography, radius, motion).
- `canvas.base`: element-level defaults (body font, border-color, code font).
- `canvas.components`: all component class styles.
- `canvas.patterns`: cross-cutting concerns (backdrops, glass surface, density, focus, scrollbar).

### Theming

- **Light/dark**: toggle the `dark` class on `<html>`.
- **Glass surface**: set `data-surface="glass"` on `<html>` for frosted-pane effects.
- **Density**: set `data-density="compact"` or `data-density="comfy"` on `<html>`.

### File structure

```
styles/
  canvas.css                # All-in-one entry point (@imports everything)
  reset.css                 # Browser normalization
  base.css                  # Element defaults
  tokens/
    colors.css              # HSL color tokens (light + dark)
    typography.css          # Font family tokens
    radius.css              # Border-radius scale
    motion.css              # Keyframes and animation tokens
  components/
    typography.css           # Type scale (.display, .h1-.h5, .p, .body, etc.)
    button.css               # .btn + variant/size classes
    input.css                # .input, .label, .field-helper
    card.css                 # .card, .card-header, .card-content, .card-footer
    badge.css                # .badge + variants, .status-badge
    separator.css            # .sep, .sep-v
    icon.css                 # [data-lucide] defaults
    avatar.css               # .avatar
    kbd.css                  # .kbd
    code-block.css           # .codeblock
    sidebar.css              # .sidebar + sub-elements
    topbar.css               # .topbar
    app-shell.css            # .app-shell, .app-main, .app-content
    stat-card.css            # .stat-card + sub-elements
    section-card.css         # .section-card + sub-elements
    data-table.css           # .dt-wrap, .dt-table, .dt-toolbar, .dt-footer
    field.css                # .field, .field-label, .field-value
    page-header.css          # .page-header + sub-elements
    empty-state.css          # .empty-card
    row-menu.css             # .rowmenu-item, .navlink
  patterns/
    backdrops.css            # Body background gradients (default + glass aurora)
    glass.css                # Glass surface frosted-pane overrides
    density.css              # Compact/comfy spacing overrides
    focus.css                # Focus pulse animation
    scrollbar.css            # Thin scrollbar styling
src/
  index.ts                   # JS utility exports
  theme.ts                   # Theme/surface/density switching
  tokens.ts                  # Token access helpers
  cn.ts                      # Class composition utility
```

## Naming conventions

- **CSS custom properties**: shadcn-compatible flat names, no `canvas-` prefix.
  Color tokens use HSL channels: `--background`, `--foreground`, `--primary`,
  `--muted-foreground`, `--border`, `--ring`, `--chart-1`, `--sidebar-background`.
  Scale tokens: `--radius-sm`, `--radius-md`, `--font-sans`, `--font-mono`.
  Animation tokens: `--animate-toast-in`, `--animate-modal-in`.
- **CSS class names**: short, flat names. No `canvas-` prefix, no BEM.
  Components: `.btn`, `.card`, `.input`, `.sidebar`, `.topbar`, `.stat-card`.
  Variants: `.btn-outline`, `.btn-destructive`, `.btn-sm`, `.badge-secondary`.
  Sub-elements: `.card-header`, `.stat-card-label`, `.dt-toolbar`, `.field-label`.
- **CSS files**: lowercase kebab-case matching the component or concern.
- **JS exports**: camelCase functions, PascalCase types.

## Development principles

1. **CSS does the work.** If something can be done in CSS, do it in CSS.
   Do not reach for JS to solve styling problems.

2. **No build step required for consumers.** The CSS files Canvas ships
   must be valid, spec-compliant CSS that a browser can consume directly
   or that any bundler can process. No custom syntax, no compile-time
   transforms.

3. **Progressive enhancement.** Use modern CSS features but ensure the
   design degrades gracefully where unsupported. Document browser support
   requirements explicitly.

4. **Minimal footprint.** Every property, every class, every file earns
   its place. A token that nothing references is dead weight.

5. **Semantic over visual.** Name things by what they mean
   (`--primary`), not what they look like (`--blue-500`). Semantic names
   survive theme changes; visual names do not.

## Toolchain

The toolchain is being rethought from scratch. Decisions should prioritize:

- Fast iteration (sub-second feedback on CSS changes).
- Correctness checks for CSS (linting, property validation).
- Size tracking (total CSS weight per module).
- Visual regression testing where practical.

Tooling choices will be documented here as they are made. Do not carry
over v2 tooling assumptions (Biome config, Vitest setup, etc.) without
explicit discussion.

## Versioning and release

- Uses [Changesets](https://github.com/changesets/changesets) for version
  management.
- v3.0.0 is the first release of the CSS-first system. This is a breaking
  change from v2.x (the React component library).
- Releases go through CI/CD. Never `npm publish` locally.

## Testing

CSS-first testing strategy (to be refined):

- **Visual regression**: screenshot comparisons of rendered component
  styles across themes and viewport sizes.
- **Token validation**: automated checks that every token is defined,
  every referenced property exists, and no orphaned tokens remain.
- **Size budget**: track per-file and total CSS size. Alert on unexpected
  growth.
- **Accessibility**: color contrast checks on token pairs, focus-visible
  styles present on interactive components.
- **JS utilities**: standard unit tests for the small JS surface area.

## Consumer contract

**Downstream component packages** (`canvas-react`, `canvas-react-native`,
`canvas-vue`, `canvas-flux`) integrate Canvas by:

1. Depending on `@olympusoss/canvas`.
2. Importing tokens and CSS for the web layer; reading token values
   programmatically for native platforms.
3. Wrapping Canvas CSS component patterns in framework-specific components.

**End-user apps** (Athena, Hera, Site, etc.) typically consume a downstream
package rather than Canvas directly. They may also import Canvas CSS or
utilities directly when needed (e.g., for custom layouts or theme setup).

When a visual decision affects all platforms (a color change, a spacing
scale adjustment, a new semantic token), it goes in Canvas. When a
decision is specific to a framework's component API (a React prop, a
React Native style object), it goes in the downstream package.

## Migration from v2

v2 was a React component library with 80+ components, Tailwind integration,
Radix primitives, Recharts, TanStack Table, TipTap, and more. v3 is a
clean break; consumers will need to rebuild their component layer using
the appropriate downstream package.

The v2 branch/tag will remain available for consumers who need to pin
while migrating.
